      "writtenOn": "2026-09-14T11:03:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What does that mean for the user? Is a refresh typically tried before the token actually expires? If not, that likely means that the user is at minimum locked out for the configured time, which might be long for a short network glitch that might even be on Gerrit\u0027s end. Could we use an exponential backoff?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 819,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 819,
        "endChar": 22
      },
      "revId": "f5f3a3af002199f65631c7703e27ce67274ca692",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fb2ac769_bafe9908",
        "filename": "Documentation/config-gerrit.txt",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 819,
      "author": {
        "id": 1011323
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-14T11:47:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Done. I changed this to exponential backoff.\n\nAfter the first transient refresh failure Gerrit retries after 1 second, then doubles the per-account in-memory delay after each failed attempt up to `auth.oauthTokenRefreshInterval` (default: 5 minutes). A successful refresh clears the backoff state, and a restart clears it as well.\n\nWhile the expired token is backed off, callers still see no usable token, but this avoids locking the user out for the full configured interval after a short outage.",
      "parentUuid": "ce6af1eb_4f4151b8",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 819,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 819,
        "endChar": 22
      },
      "revId": "f5f3a3af002199f65631c7703e27ce67274ca692",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
